<h2 id="get-started">How to start a side hustle in Atlanta this month</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Choose your zone.</strong> Atlanta is huge. Pick a home base, such as intown neighborhoods, the Perimeter or the northern suburbs, and keep most work within 20 minutes of it.</li>
  <li><strong>Pick one primary hustle.</strong> Match it to the hours you can protect every week. Not sure? Try the <a href="/tools/side-hustle-quiz/">side hustle quiz</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Handle the paperwork.</strong> If you live inside city limits, apply for an Occupational Tax Certificate. Set up a separate checking account for side income.</li>
  <li><strong>Get visible locally.</strong> Post on Nextdoor and neighborhood Facebook groups, and set up a free Google Business Profile for service work.</li>
  <li><strong>Mark the calendar.</strong> Add home games, the Peachtree Road Race, Music Midtown, Dragon Con and convention weeks so you can plan surge shifts.</li>
  <li><strong>Collect three reviews.</strong> Early reviews matter more than any ad. A small first-job discount is fine if it gets you started.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="georgia-taxes">Georgia taxes and Atlanta rules for side income</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>State income tax:</strong> Georgia uses a flat rate of 5.19% for 2026, with further cuts scheduled in later years if revenue targets are met.</li>
  <li><strong>Self-employment tax:</strong> 15.3% federal on net profit for Social Security and Medicare, on top of federal income tax. The <a href="/tools/self-employment-tax-calculator/">self-employment tax calculator</a> estimates your set-aside.</li>
  <li><strong>Atlanta business license:</strong> businesses operating inside city limits, including home-based ones, generally need an Occupational Tax Certificate from the city's Office of Revenue. Fees are tied to gross receipts.</li>
  <li><strong>Short-term rentals:</strong> the City of Atlanta requires a short-term rental license with an annual fee. Check current rules before hosting or co-hosting, since the ordinance has been revised.</li>
  <li><strong>Sales tax:</strong> if you sell physical goods, register with the Georgia Department of Revenue through the Georgia Tax Center. The state rate is 4%, and local taxes push the total higher in Atlanta. Most personal services aren't taxed.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><strong>Quick tip:</strong> Apps send 1099 forms, but it's on you to track deductions. Log every business mile, plus phone costs and supplies. Those deductions reduce both income tax and self-employment tax.</div>

